<h2>Layer 0 — <code>cw_attrs</code> / <code>cw_presenter</code>, bring your own markup</h2>

<p>
  The only helper crosswire includes globally. Note the element structure below is
  completely different from what the shipped partial renders and the controller still
  works — it only ever knew targets and values, never markup.
</p>

<section id="layer0-disclosure">
  <% d = cw_presenter(:disclosure, id: "layer0", open: true, region: true) %>
  <details <%= cw_attrs(d.root_attrs) %> open>
    <summary <%= cw_attrs(d.trigger_attrs) %>>Native details, crosswire wiring</summary>
    <div <%= cw_attrs(d.panel_attrs) %>>
      Same presenter, no partial, no helper beyond <code>cw_attrs</code>.
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="layer0-merge">
  <h3>Merging several presenters onto one element</h3>
  <% dismiss = cw_presenter(:dismiss, escape: true) %>
  <% transition = cw_presenter(:transition, leave: "fade", leave_to: "opacity-0") %>
  <div <%= cw_attrs(dismiss.root_attrs, transition.root_attrs, transition.leave_on, class: "flash") %>>
    Two controllers, one element, one merged <code>data-controller</code>.
    <button <%= cw_attrs(dismiss.trigger_attrs) %>>&times;</button>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="layer0-escaping">
  <h3>Escaping</h3>
  <%# `->` in a data-action is HTML-escaped to `-&gt;` on the way out and decoded by
      the parser on the way in. Correct, and the reason the integration suite asserts
      on parsed DOM attributes rather than on raw markup. %>
  <button <%= cw_attrs(cw_presenter(:dialog, id: "escaping-demo").trigger_attrs) %>>Open</button>
</section>
